# decision SwiftPM owns one Package dependency expression

The Swift adapter edits one `.package(url:..., revision:...)` expression in the top-level
`Package(dependencies:)` array. Locked dependencies use `revision`; floating dependencies use
`branch`. The export name identifies status output; SwiftPM derives package identity from the
Git URL. `Package.resolved` remains downstream because resolving can rewrite unrelated pins.
Only a `dependencies:` argument at the outer `Package(...)` call depth is eligible; target
dependency arrays with the same label are never edited. Insertions are delimited and remember
an added separator, empty array, or newly created top-level argument so unwire restores the
original Swift source bytes.

# invariant Source moves remove the old Swift identity

When `set --git` changes a dependency source, the transaction unwires adapters using the old
source before wiring the new source. This prevents two Swift package identities from remaining
in `Package.swift`, and remains atomic with every other ecosystem.

# test Swift adapter

- Fixture/golden wiring is idempotent and preserves products, targets, comments, and unrelated
  package dependencies.
- Drift checks the normalized URL and locked revision.
- Unwire and source moves remove the old package expression only.
